1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,387
Five friends.

2
00:00:01,387 --> 00:00:02,287
Two badges.

3
00:00:02,232 --> 00:00:03,408
One says captain.

4
00:00:03,408 --> 00:00:05,200
The other says vice captain.

5
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,658
How many ways can the
two badges be given out?

6
00:00:08,658 --> 00:00:10,202
Most people guess ten.

7
00:00:10,202 --> 00:00:11,998
The real answer is twenty.

8
00:00:12,448 --> 00:00:15,212
Diya watched this happen at
her class election today.

9
00:00:15,562 --> 00:00:17,321
Meera as captain, Sana as vice.

10
00:00:17,321 --> 00:00:19,244
Or Sana as captain, Meera as vice.

11
00:00:19,244 --> 00:00:20,163
Same two girls.

12
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:21,525
So why count them twice?

13
00:00:22,025 --> 00:00:23,859
Because the two badges are different.

14
00:00:23,859 --> 00:00:25,954
When order matters, we count permutations.

15
00:00:26,604 --> 00:00:28,349
So what is a permutation?

16
00:00:28,699 --> 00:00:33,529
A permutation is an arrangement in a
definite order of a number of objects,

17
00:00:33,529 --> 00:00:35,638
taken some or all at a time.

18
00:00:36,038 --> 00:00:38,755
Arrangement means putting
things into places.

19
00:00:38,755 --> 00:00:42,601
Definite order means first place
and second place are different.

20
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,216
Some or all means we may fill
a few places, or every place.

21
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:48,853
Side by side.

22
00:00:48,853 --> 00:00:51,465
Meera is captain, Sana is vice captain.

23
00:00:51,465 --> 00:00:52,895
Now swap the badges.

24
00:00:53,295 --> 00:00:55,859
Same two people, but
each has a different job.

25
00:00:55,859 --> 00:00:57,956
So these are two different arrangements.

26
00:00:58,556 --> 00:00:59,491
Now you try.

27
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:03,645
A lock code one, two, three,
and a lock code three, two, one.

28
00:01:03,645 --> 00:01:04,987
Same, or different?

29
00:01:04,987 --> 00:01:06,148
Pause and decide.

30
00:01:09,348 --> 00:01:10,248
Different!

31
00:01:10,061 --> 00:01:12,421
The lock opens only in the right order.

32
00:01:12,421 --> 00:01:14,421
So a lock code is a permutation.

33
00:01:15,071 --> 00:01:16,446
Let us count every way.

34
00:01:16,446 --> 00:01:18,815
Five friends down the
side are the captains.

35
00:01:19,215 --> 00:01:22,025
The same five across the
top are the vice captains.

36
00:01:22,425 --> 00:01:23,991
Each square is one pair.

37
00:01:23,991 --> 00:01:26,425
Ravi captain, Meera vice is one square.

38
00:01:26,925 --> 00:01:29,346
But one person cannot wear both badges.

39
00:01:29,346 --> 00:01:32,294
So the squares on this
diagonal are crossed out.

40
00:01:32,894 --> 00:01:36,220
Five rows, and four good
squares in each row.

41
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:37,523
Five times four.

42
00:01:37,523 --> 00:01:39,261
Twenty ways, not ten.

43
00:01:39,911 --> 00:01:41,147
Now the shortcut.

44
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:42,935
Think of two empty places.

45
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:44,422
The first is captain.

46
00:01:44,422 --> 00:01:46,867
Any of the five friends can take it.

47
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:47,852
Five choices.

48
00:01:48,602 --> 00:01:50,941
The second place is vice captain.

49
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:53,125
One friend is already used up.

50
00:01:53,125 --> 00:01:54,989
So four choices are left.

51
00:01:55,739 --> 00:01:59,990
The fundamental principle of
counting says: multiply the choices.

52
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:01,890
Five times four is twenty.

53
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:04,122
Now make it general.

54
00:02:04,122 --> 00:02:07,762
Say we have n different
things, and r places to fill.

55
00:02:08,512 --> 00:02:10,746
The first place has n choices.

56
00:02:10,746 --> 00:02:12,822
The second has n minus one.

57
00:02:12,822 --> 00:02:14,845
The third has n minus two.

58
00:02:15,595 --> 00:02:17,794
Each place uses up one thing.

59
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:22,694
So the last place, place number
r, has n minus r plus one choices.

60
00:02:23,444 --> 00:02:24,813
Multiply them all.

61
00:02:24,813 --> 00:02:27,048
This product is written n P r.

62
00:02:27,048 --> 00:02:29,107
The P stands for permutation.

63
00:02:29,857 --> 00:02:30,934
Watch it grow.

64
00:02:30,934 --> 00:02:33,584
Five friends, one more place at a time.

65
00:02:33,984 --> 00:02:35,746
One place, five ways.

66
00:02:35,746 --> 00:02:37,337
Two places, twenty.

67
00:02:37,337 --> 00:02:38,981
Three places, sixty.

68
00:02:39,381 --> 00:02:41,895
Four places, one hundred and twenty.

69
00:02:41,895 --> 00:02:44,770
Five places, still one hundred and twenty.

70
00:02:45,370 --> 00:02:46,270
Quick one.

71
00:02:46,106 --> 00:02:48,900
Why did the count stop
growing at the last place?

72
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:49,941
Pause and think.

73
00:02:53,141 --> 00:02:56,644
When four places are filled,
only one friend is left.

74
00:02:56,644 --> 00:02:57,544
One choice.

75
00:02:57,539 --> 00:02:59,534
And times one changes nothing.

76
00:03:00,184 --> 00:03:03,648
There is a neater way to
write it, with factorials.

77
00:03:03,998 --> 00:03:08,707
n factorial means n times every
whole number below it, down to one.

78
00:03:08,707 --> 00:03:11,566
Five factorial is one hundred and twenty.

79
00:03:12,316 --> 00:03:15,701
Our product stops early,
at n minus r plus one.

80
00:03:15,701 --> 00:03:19,034
The part that is missing
is n minus r factorial.

81
00:03:19,784 --> 00:03:21,691
So we divide it away.

82
00:03:21,691 --> 00:03:25,933
n P r equals n factorial,
over n minus r factorial.

83
00:03:26,683 --> 00:03:27,583
Check it.

84
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:32,336
Five P two is one hundred and twenty
over three factorial, which is six.

85
00:03:32,336 --> 00:03:33,676
That gives twenty.

86
00:03:34,326 --> 00:03:35,422
Remember this.

87
00:03:35,422 --> 00:03:40,404
When order matters, n P r counts the
ways to fill r places from n different

88
00:03:40,404 --> 00:03:41,304
things.

89
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:43,471
Now, one worked example.

90
00:03:43,821 --> 00:03:45,137
Eight runners race.

91
00:03:45,137 --> 00:03:48,179
Gold, silver and bronze
go to the first three.

92
00:03:48,179 --> 00:03:50,888
In how many ways can the medals be given?

93
00:03:51,388 --> 00:03:52,288
Step one.

94
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:55,450
Gold is not the same as
silver, so order matters.

95
00:03:55,450 --> 00:03:56,981
This is a permutation.

96
00:03:57,731 --> 00:03:58,631
Step two.

97
00:03:58,540 --> 00:03:59,960
n is eight runners.

98
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:01,331
r is three medals.

99
00:04:02,081 --> 00:04:03,038
Step three.

100
00:04:03,038 --> 00:04:06,616
Eight P three is eight
factorial over five factorial.

101
00:04:07,366 --> 00:04:08,266
Step four.

102
00:04:08,262 --> 00:04:12,957
Everything from five down cancels,
leaving eight times seven times six.

103
00:04:13,707 --> 00:04:16,446
So there are three hundred
and thirty six ways.

104
00:04:17,096 --> 00:04:18,557
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:04:18,907 --> 00:04:21,557
Many students write eight
times eight times eight.

106
00:04:21,557 --> 00:04:23,370
That is five hundred and twelve.

107
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,075
But one runner cannot win two medals.

108
00:04:26,075 --> 00:04:28,489
n to the power r is only for repeats.

109
00:04:28,489 --> 00:04:30,939
Here it is eight times seven times six.

110
00:04:31,589 --> 00:04:32,811
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:32,811 --> 00:04:36,567
In JEE Main, as questions that build
numbers from given digits, with no

112
00:04:36,567 --> 00:04:37,485
digit repeated.

113
00:04:38,085 --> 00:04:39,078
Exam level now.

114
00:04:39,078 --> 00:04:42,941
How many four digit even numbers can
be made from these six digits, with

115
00:04:42,941 --> 00:04:43,841
no repeats?

116
00:04:43,715 --> 00:04:44,750
Pause and solve.

117
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:51,545
The answer is B.

118
00:04:51,545 --> 00:04:53,545
One hundred and fifty six.

119
00:04:54,045 --> 00:04:57,361
Even means the last digit
is zero, two or four.

120
00:04:57,361 --> 00:05:01,130
Zero last: the rest take
five, four and three choices.

121
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:02,030
Sixty.

122
00:05:02,243 --> 00:05:04,116
Now put two or four last.

123
00:05:04,116 --> 00:05:05,616
That is two choices.

124
00:05:06,116 --> 00:05:09,301
The first digit cannot be
zero or the last digit.

125
00:05:09,301 --> 00:05:10,285
Four choices.

126
00:05:10,285 --> 00:05:11,723
Then four and three.

127
00:05:11,723 --> 00:05:14,455
Two times four times twelve is ninety six.

128
00:05:14,955 --> 00:05:18,547
Sixty plus ninety six is
one hundred and fifty six.

129
00:05:19,147 --> 00:05:20,687
Here is your recap.

130
00:05:20,687 --> 00:05:22,270
Screenshot this card.

131
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:27,656
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

132
00:05:28,056 --> 00:05:28,956
One.

133
00:05:28,498 --> 00:05:30,955
Why is swapping the two badges a new way?

134
00:05:31,855 --> 00:05:32,755
Two.

135
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:33,652
Find seven P two.

136
00:05:34,552 --> 00:05:35,452
Three.

137
00:05:35,129 --> 00:05:37,828
Why is five P four equal to five P five?

138
00:05:38,928 --> 00:05:40,982
Next time: APPLE has two P's.

139
00:05:40,982 --> 00:05:43,125
Swap them, and nothing changes.

140
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:45,783
So how many words can APPLE really make?
